= Readable depth and localization =

'''Status: Accepted editorial/navigation direction; three-surface implementation begins with Wave 0.'''

BITwiki localizes knowledge primarily by '''reader depth and technicality''', while preserving one semantic identity for each concept.

== Principle ==

'''One concept, three reader surfaces.'''

A mature public concept should normally be addressable at three depths:

<pre>
Title/Simple
    ↓
Title
    ↓
Title/Technical
</pre>

* <code>Title/Simple</code> — Simple English / low-prerequisite orientation.
* <code>Title</code> — Core article; the canonical general treatment, roughly Wikipedia/Scholarpedia depth.
* <code>Title/Technical</code> — advanced/formal/practitioner treatment.

These are three presentations of one concept, not three competing knowledge objects.

== Why separate surfaces? ==

A single long article can technically contain simple and advanced sections, but it often forces a beginner to navigate around material written for a different audience. Separately addressable depth surfaces allow:

* direct links for a known reader level;
* depth-preserving navigation between related concepts;
* different pedagogical sequencing without changing factual identity;
* technical depth without overwhelming the Core article;
* Simple English treatment without deleting necessary advanced content.

The trade-off is duplication risk. The capture → compile/write → audit workflow exists partly to prevent the three surfaces from drifting into contradictory truth stores.

== Depth definitions ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Depth !! Function !! Typical content
|-
| '''Simple''' || Immediate orientation for a curious general reader || plain-language definition; why it matters; familiar examples; minimal prerequisites; essential vocabulary explained on first use
|-
| '''Core''' || Durable working understanding || definitions; scope; mechanisms; history where relevant; major distinctions; representative examples; applications; evidence; limitations; references
|-
| '''Technical''' || Practitioner / advanced-study / research depth || formal definitions; equations; algorithms; implementation details; standards; protocols; edge cases; competing models; design trade-offs; open technical questions
|}

'''Frontier / research is not a fourth reading depth.''' Emerging evidence, open problems, disputed claims, and active research belong in explicit sections inside Core or Technical pages.

== Canonical title convention ==

For a concept whose canonical title is <code>Ontology</code>:

<pre>
Ontology             → Core
Ontology/Simple      → Simple
Ontology/Technical   → Technical
</pre>

The unsuffixed title owns the canonical public concept name. The suffixed pages are depth-localized reader surfaces.

The Core page owns the concept's canonical `Knowledge object` identity and domain/category membership. Simple and Technical derivatives should not duplicate those assertions merely to satisfy navigation or CI. This prevents category listings and semantic queries from treating one concept as three independent subjects.

This convention uses ordinary MediaWiki titles and does not require a custom Simple or Technical namespace.

== Depth-aware linking ==

BITwiki should hyperlink concepts densely in normal prose.

Core pages link to Core:

<pre>
[[Computation]]
[[Programming language]]
[[Causality]]
</pre>

Simple pages should prefer another concept's Simple surface when it exists:

<pre>
[[Computation/Simple|computation]]
</pre>

If no Simple page exists, link to Core rather than create an empty surface.

Technical pages should prefer Technical where it exists and otherwise fall back to Core.

Depth-preserving links are a reader-navigation rule, not an ontological relation.

== Hyperlinks versus semantic relationships ==

MediaWiki links and SMW relations do different jobs.

<pre>
[[Algorithm]]
</pre>

means the target concept is useful in the reader's present context.

<pre>
[[Depends on::Computation]]
</pre>

asserts a stronger machine-readable semantic relationship.

Do not create SMW assertions merely because two pages link to each other.

== Shared factual identity ==

Depth changes explanation, not the underlying claim.

A Simple page may omit equations or historical nuance, but it must not:

* replace uncertainty with false certainty;
* turn a contested interpretation into settled fact;
* introduce a different definition merely because it is easier to explain;
* contradict Core or Technical;
* cite weaker evidence for a claim that has stronger canonical support.

When simplification requires qualification, preserve the qualification in simple language.

== Localization versus translation ==

Language translation remains a separate dimension.

<pre>
language / locale
≠ reading depth
≠ technical depth
≠ epistemic status
≠ Domain
</pre>

A Spanish Technical page is still Technical. A Simple English page can still be rigorous.

== Required surfaces ==

For foundational public concepts:

* '''Core''' is mandatory.
* '''Simple''' is expected.
* '''Technical''' is expected when meaningful technical/formal depth exists.

Wave-based context compilation may still conclude that a Technical surface would only duplicate Core. Such an exception should be explicit rather than silently generating filler.

== Workflow ==

Depth surfaces are produced together through a three-state wave workflow:

<pre>
Context capture
    ↓
Context compilation + writing
    ↓
Context audit
</pre>

Research is captured across the whole concept wave first. Definitions and relationships are then compiled consistently before any of the three depth surfaces are written. The final audit checks factual, citation, link, semantic, and cross-depth consistency.

== Portal behavior ==

Portals should expose the same progression:

* '''Start simply''' — Simple surfaces and low-prerequisite concepts;
* '''Foundations''' — prerequisite concepts;
* '''Core map''' — canonical Core pages;
* '''Methods / tools''' — how work is done;
* '''Technical''' — formal, implementation, standards, tooling, datasets;
* '''Research''' — open or disputed material already represented inside relevant pages.

== Editorial tests ==

For every concept ask:

# Does Simple explain the real concept without manufacturing certainty?
# Does Core stand alone as the normal public article?
# Does Technical add real formal/practitioner depth rather than repeat Core?
# Do the three surfaces agree on definitions, scope, and evidentiary standing?
# Do links preserve reader depth where useful?
# Are prerequisites exposed instead of silently assumed?
# Are open/disputed matters visibly qualified?

